content:
To migrate a flat Pulumi TypeScript program to a `ComponentResource` **without replacing existing resources**, you need to preserve each child resource’s **logical identity** by adding **`aliases`** that point to the old URNs.

## Why replacement happens

In a flat program, a resource URN looks roughly like:

```text
urn:pulumi:dev::project::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::assets
```

After moving that resource under a component, its URN becomes something like:

```text
urn:pulumi:dev::project::my:module:WebApp$aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::assets
```

Even if the physical AWS resource is unchanged, Pulumi sees the URN change and thinks it is a different resource unless you provide an alias.

---

# Migration pattern

## Before: flat program

```ts
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

const bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", {
  versioning: {
    enabled: true,
  },
  serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
    rule: {
      applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
        sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
      },
    },
  },
});

const db = new aws.rds.Instance("db", {
  engine: "postgres",
  instanceClass: "db.t3.micro",
  allocatedStorage: 20,
  username: "app",
  password: "example-password",
  skipFinalSnapshot: true,
});
```

---

## After: wrapped in a component

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

export interface WebAppArgs {
  dbPassword: pulumi.Input<string>;
}

export class WebApp extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly bucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
  public readonly db: aws.rds.Instance;

  constructor(name: string, args: WebAppArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("custom:app:WebApp", name, {}, opts);

    this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", {
      versioning: {
        enabled: true,
      },
      serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
        rule: {
          applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
            sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
          },
        },
      },
    }, {
      parent: this,
      aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
    });

    this.db = new aws.rds.Instance("db", {
      engine: "postgres",
      instanceClass: "db.t3.micro",
      allocatedStorage: 20,
      username: "app",
      password: args.dbPassword,
      skipFinalSnapshot: true,
    }, {
      parent: this,
      aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
    });

    this.registerOutputs();
  }
}
```

Then in your main program:

```ts
const config = new pulumi.Config();
const dbPassword = config.requireSecret("dbPassword");

const app = new WebApp("webapp", {
  dbPassword,
});
```

---

# Key idea

This is the important part:

```ts
aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]
```

That tells Pulumi:

> “This resource used to live directly under the stack root. Now it lives under this component. Treat it as the same resource.”

Without that alias, Pulumi will plan:
- create new child resource under component
- delete old flat resource

With the alias, Pulumi will instead plan:
- **same resource**, just adopted under the component

---

# If the resource name also changed

If you changed both the parent and the name, include both in the alias.

Example: old resource was `"assets"`, new one is `"app-assets"`.

```ts
this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("app-assets", args, {
  parent: this,
  aliases: [{
    name: "assets",
    parent: pulumi.rootStackResource,
  }],
});
```

---

# If the component name changed too

Usually child aliases only need to refer to the **old child identity**, which in the flat case means root parent.

If you are migrating from one component structure to another nested structure, you may need aliases referencing old parent chains.

For flat → component, this is usually enough:

```ts
aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]
```

---

# Full dev/prod-style example

Given your canonical scenario, you might refactor a flat stack into a component like this:

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

interface WebInfraArgs {
  env: "dev" | "prod";
  dbPassword: pulumi.Input<string>;
}

export class WebInfra extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
  public readonly bucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
  public readonly db: aws.rds.Instance;

  constructor(name: string, args: WebInfraArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("custom:infra:WebInfra", name, {}, opts);

    const isProd = args.env === "prod";

    this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", {
      versioning: {
        enabled: true,
      },
      serverSideEncryptionConfiguration: {
        rule: {
          applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
            sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
          },
        },
      },
      acl: "private",
    }, {
      parent: this,
      aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
    });

    this.db = new aws.rds.Instance("db", {
      engine: "postgres",
      instanceClass: isProd ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
      allocatedStorage: 20,
      username: "app",
      password: args.dbPassword,
      multiAz: isProd,
      storageEncrypted: true,
      deletionProtection: true,
      backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
      publiclyAccessible: false,
      skipFinalSnapshot: false,
    }, {
      parent: this,
      aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
    });

    this.registerOutputs({
      bucketName: this.bucket.bucket,
      dbAddress: this.db.address,
    });
  }
}
```

Main program:

```ts
const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const env = pulumi.getStack() as "dev" | "prod";
const dbPassword = cfg.requireSecret("dbPassword");

new WebInfra("web", {
  env,
  dbPassword,
});
```

---

# Important migration checklist

## 1. Keep child resource names the same
If old code used:

```ts
new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", ...)
```

keep `"assets"` unless you also alias the old name.

---

## 2. Add `parent: this`
Otherwise the resource is still flat and not really inside the component.

---

## 3. Add alias for old root parent
For flat → component:

```ts
aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]
```

---

## 4. Do not change resource type tokens during the same migration
For example, avoid changing:
- `aws.s3.Bucket` → `aws.s3.BucketV2`
- `aws.iam.RolePolicyAttachment` → some other resource form

Do the structural refactor first, then type changes later.

---

## 5. Preview first
Run:

```bash
pulumi preview
```

You want to see **same/update** operations, not create/delete replacements.

---

# Multiple resources helper pattern

If you have many resources, you can DRY the alias logic:

```ts
const adoptedFromRoot: pulumi.ResourceOptions = {
  parent: this,
  aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
};
```

Then:

```ts
this.bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", { ... }, adoptedFromRoot);
this.db = new aws.rds.Instance("db", { ... }, adoptedFromRoot);
```

If you need to merge options safely:

```ts
function adoptFromRoot(parent: pulumi.Resource): pulumi.ResourceOptions {
  return {
    parent,
    aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
  };
}
```

---

# Common pitfall: alias on component only

Adding an alias to the component itself does **not** automatically preserve child URNs.

This is wrong/incomplete for flat → component migration:

```ts
super("custom:app:WebApp", name, {}, {
  aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }],
});
```

You need aliases on the **moved child resources**.

---

# When you may need explicit URN aliasing

Usually parent/name aliases are enough. But if migration gets more complex, you can alias by URN directly:

```ts
aliases: [{
  urn: "urn:pulumi:dev::project::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::assets"
}]
```

This is less portable across stacks, so prefer structural aliases unless necessary.

---

# Recommended migration strategy

1. Refactor code into a component.
2. Keep all existing child resource names unchanged.
3. Add `parent: this` to every moved resource.
4. Add `aliases: [{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }]` to every moved resource.
5. `pulumi preview`
6. Confirm no replacements.
7. `pulumi up`

